  [PASS] sum rho_tilde != 1 + eps (printed is wrong)   |sum - (1+eps)| = 1.000e-01
  => T2 follows: the (1+eps) normaliser in p_0 = lambda/(1+eps) (p.26)
     has no source; Eq. (107) and the Thm 6 proof both give p_0 = lambda.

==============================================================================
T3  sign of Eq. (111)-(112)  [equal-share transfer]
==============================================================================
  Eq. (97) defines the insured loss as  L - r,  but Eq. (113) flattens
  L + r. Only one sign can be right. Both satisfy clearing Eq. (95),
  which is why the paper's own check does not catch it.
  [WARN] insurance box active for 11 agent(s) -- gap values are not trustworthy
  as printed  r = -L + l/N + d
      clearing Eq.(95) max|sum_k r_k| = 1.164e-10   (both signs clear -- this is the trap)
      (UM) max relative gap           = 1.205e+00
  [WARN] insurance box active for 11 agent(s) -- gap values are not trustworthy
  corrected   r = +L - l/N - d
      clearing Eq.(95) max|sum_k r_k| = 1.164e-10   (both signs clear -- this is the trap)
      (UM) max relative gap           = 6.086e-13
  [PASS] corrected sign supports (UM)   max rel gap = 6.086e-13  (solver tolerance)
  [PASS] printed sign does NOT support (UM)   max rel gap = 1.205e+00
  [PASS] the two signs separate by >= 3 orders of magnitude   ratio = 1.980e+12
  => Eq. (111)-(112) should read  r_k = +L_k - l/(n+m) - delta_k,
     and Eq. (113) should read    L_k - r_k = l/(n+m) + delta_k.
     Eq. (122) in Theorem 9 already uses this corrected sign.

==============================================================================
T5  Theorem 9: is  t* := sum_k t_k  circular?  [INVESTIGATED -- NOT AN ERROR]
==============================================================================
  Eq. (122) sets  r_k = L_k - t_k - w_k (l(w) - t*)  with t* := sum_k t_k,
  while t_k also names each agent's own VaR variable. That looks circular:
  one might expect self-consistency to force t* = VaR_bmin(l*), the
  PLANNER's VaR. We tested both readings. They give the SAME objective,
  because a constant shift of r_k is exactly objective-neutral:
      d/dc [ (1-eps) E[u+r+c] - q.(r+c) - eps CVaR(L-r-c) ]
          = (1-eps) - sum_w q_w + eps = 1 - sum_w q_w = 0,
  which holds PRECISELY BECAUSE sum_w rho_tilde_w = 1 (test T1). So the
  {t_k} in (122) are free constants, t* := sum_k t_k is a harmless
  normalisation, and Theorem 9's construction is sound as printed.
  (Had the paper's sum = 1 + eps been right, this invariance would fail.)
  beta_min = 0.9,  |S_>| = 10 of 11 agents
  sum_k t_k (as printed) = -147545.1679
  VaR_bmin(l*)  (planner) = -201892.6044
  these differ by 54347.4364 -- the two readings of t*
  [WARN] insurance box active for 11 agent(s) -- gap values are not trustworthy
  [WARN] insurance box active for 11 agent(s) -- gap values are not trustworthy
  as printed  (t* = sum_k t_k):   clearing = 6.185e-11   (UM) max rel gap = 1.997e-12
  corrected   (sum_k t_k = t*_P): clearing = 6.912e-11   (UM) max rel gap = 1.998e-12
  [PASS] Theorem 9 construction supports (UM)  [t* = sum_k t_k, as printed]   max rel gap = 1.997e-12
  [PASS] both readings of t* agree (construction is shift-invariant)   |difference| = 1.170e-15
  VERDICT: not an error. Theorem 9's construction is verified under the
  (UM) test, which D4 in the first experiment round never actually ran --
  D4 checked only Eq. (95) and Eq. (121), which hold for ANY {t_k}.
  Constant-shift coefficient 1 - sum_w q_w = 0.000e+00

==============================================================================
T8  Remark 11: heterogeneous eps reduces to a planner at (eps_min, beta_P)
==============================================================================
  Claim: with phi_k = eps_k beta_k/(1-beta_k), the binding bounds on q are
    q_w >= (1-eps_min) rho_w   and   q_w <= (1+phi_min) rho_w,
  which are exactly a planner's bounds at eps_P = eps_min and
  beta_P = phi_min/(eps_min + phi_min).  The paper defers this; we check it.
  eps_k in [0.2, 0.5], beta_k in [0.9, 0.99]
  eps_min = 0.2000   phi_min = 4.5000   => beta_P = 0.957447
  [PASS] beta_P falls strictly outside the agents' own {beta_k} (sharp test)   beta_P = 0.957447, pool = [0.9, 0.99]
  [PASS] planner's phi_P equals phi_min (algebra of Remark 11)   |phi_P - phi_min| = 7.105e-15
  max lower-bound violation  max_k,w [(1-eps_k)rho_w - q_w] = 0.000e+00
  max upper-bound violation  max_k,w [q_w - (1+phi_k)rho_w] = 0.000e+00
  [PASS] q from the (eps_min, beta_P) planner is feasible for EVERY agent   tol = 1.127e-09
  [PASS] sum_w q_w == 1   |sum q - 1| = 2.220e-16

